WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:07.950
The Luria-Delbrück experiment excited many biologists,

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:13.380
but there was skepticism because of the unfamiliarity 

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:18.130
with the methodology. And Joshua Lederberg was 

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:22.780
a budding young bacteriologist who developed a new method 

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:28.110
that could allow him to validate the conclusions of Luria-Delbrück with a method that was much more 

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:32.000
familiar to the rest of the biologists, and therefore could be accepted by them,

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:33.800
as you know,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.740
an approach they could understand. And what he basically figured out was 

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:44.070
that you could actually make prints of bacteria.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:48.550
And the simple concept is you spread bacteria onto a Petri dish,

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.820
and then they will grow up generating progeny on those plates.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.380
You could transfer some of those progeny to a velvet, and then 

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:03.690
place another bacterium on top of the velvet and transfer the bacterium from the velvet onto the new 

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:04.250
Petri plates.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.550
So it's very much like a printing press, where you have a master block and you put ink on it,

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.800
and then you take that block and some of the ink gets transferred to the first page, and some of the ink gets transferred to the 

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.590
second page, and the third page. And that technique is called replica plating.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.550
So you could take bacterium that were spread on the plate,

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:25.110
lots of them,

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:29.080
and you could now transfer some of their progeny to a plate,

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:30.010
which had,

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:31.140
for example,

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:36.180
T1 spread on it. In the spontaneous mutation model, the prediction would be 

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.430
that if there was a bacterium on the master that was T1 resistant,

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:44.460
some of its progeny would be transferred to the plate in exactly the same place on each of the plates.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:45.440
So again,

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:46.670
by the printing press analogy,

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:51.050
if you have over here on the printing press you have and E,

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.260
and it's got some ink on it,

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:56.990
and you put four different pieces of paper onto the master,

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:02.173
the E will appear at the exact same place on each of the replicas because the E was 

00:02:02.343 --> 00:02:04.013
preexisting in the master.

00:02:04.863 --> 00:02:07.343
And so that is the very simple concept.

00:02:07.343 --> 00:02:11.273
If the spontaneous mutation had occurred prior to any exposure to T1,

00:02:11.573 --> 00:02:14.843
there should be progeny all together on a particular part of the plate,

00:02:15.233 --> 00:02:17.423
and I will transfer them to three different plates,

00:02:17.693 --> 00:02:19.403
the new colony should appear,

00:02:19.793 --> 00:02:23.043
the new resistant cell should appear in exactly the same place on all the plates.

00:02:23.823 --> 00:02:27.893
Lederberg predicted a very different outcome if the mutations were being induced.

00:02:28.533 --> 00:02:33.343
In that experiment, transferring cells from the master to the plate 

00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:38.743
with T1, some random cells somewhere on the plate will change to T1 

00:02:38.963 --> 00:02:42.443
resistance on that plate and give rise to a colony.

00:02:44.073 --> 00:02:45.843
Those same cells,

00:02:45.843 --> 00:02:50.773
when transferred to another plate, a resistant cell will appear randomly someplace else on the 

00:02:50.783 --> 00:02:51.283
plate.

00:02:51.293 --> 00:02:56.193
So resistant colonies should not be at the same place on the different 

00:02:56.193 --> 00:03:01.113
plates because it was not predetermined by whether there was a resistant cell the 

00:03:01.123 --> 00:03:02.143
existing master.

